WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.677
Film Courage: What does it mean&nbsp;
to be an international bestseller?

00:00:02.677 --> 00:00:06.840
Andrew Warren, Author: For me there's no direct,&nbsp;
I don't think there's any specific qualification,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:12.840
but for me I didn't want to use a term like&nbsp;
that unless I could back it up. I hit the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:20.700
seller lists on Amazon for my category which&nbsp;
there's various categories on Amazon. I tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.500
to show up in categories like spy thriller,&nbsp;
assassin thrillers, political thrillers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:32.340
those kinds of categories. I hit number one more&nbsp;
so Amazon will award you a bestseller tag if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:37.380
hit number one and stay there for a certain amount&nbsp;
of time. I don't know if anyone knows how long it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:42.120
is. Maybe there's a set amount of time. I have&nbsp;
a feeling it's probably dependent on the market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.920
and how many books Amazon is selling at the time&nbsp;
but just because you hit number one doesn't mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.600
you get that tag. You have to hit number one and&nbsp;
stay there for a certain amount of time. When my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.240
books had hit number one and gotten that tag in&nbsp;
multiple countries that was when I was like well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:06.540
I guess I'm an international best-selling author&nbsp;
so and so that was for Red Phoenix no that was for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:16.500
let's see Tokyo Black hit the best sellers list&nbsp;
in Canada and a cold kill I believe hit the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:22.140
seller list in America another book called depth&nbsp;
chart which is when I co-wrote that also hit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:30.240
best seller list in America and the the boxed set&nbsp;
of the first three books that also got the when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:38.280
say hit the best seller list I mean it earned the&nbsp;
Amazon bestseller tag so and so America Australia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:43.860
you are Canada America Australia those are&nbsp;
the three territories where I got it so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.980
what amount of time did that take to happen&nbsp;
it was over the course of years I mean for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:52.920
sure it wasn't all at once because those&nbsp;
books were released at different times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:59.760
yeah I was kind of like I didn't really&nbsp;
think about it I was super stoked to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:06.000
those bestseller tags of course but at the end&nbsp;
of the day they don't mean as much I mean they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.260
draw the reader's eye to the book and of&nbsp;
course they're indicative of success but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:15.660
like I said for example Red Phoenix which was by&nbsp;
far my most successful book did not earn one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.820
those tags it for whatever reason like maybe it&nbsp;
was number two in its category I don't know but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:27.540
but that book was my most financially successful&nbsp;
and it didn't earn it so I mean I don't think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.760
I wouldn't really like advise like chasing&nbsp;
things like that and I mean sure they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.840
nice when they happen and of course they can be&nbsp;
beneficial but I don't think there's beneficial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:43.560
as people think I've heard a lot of interviews&nbsp;
with really successful self-published authors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:50.220
like people that are making like millions at it&nbsp;
and they've they've kind of done analyzes over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.100
how much of a sales bump do you get when you're&nbsp;
the New York Times bestseller versus just being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.900
a successful book and it's not as much as you&nbsp;
would think so I certainly wouldn't let that hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:06.900
you back being afraid oh I'm not going to hit the&nbsp;
best seller list I don't think it's as important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:12.360
as people believe it is maybe recommended in&nbsp;
search more yeah it's possible like I said I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.880
sure it helps a little but if you don't hit it&nbsp;
that does not mean your book is a failure sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:24.540
do you ever go on Goodreads and and as an author&nbsp;
and I see sometimes authors interacting and do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.580
you ever I do I not as much as I used to mostly&nbsp;
because I find my readers don't use it as much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.640
I do use it and some I think certain communities&nbsp;
are much more active like I definitely think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.660
romance community is super active on there so&nbsp;
you kind of got to go where your readers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:50.100
I do have my own Facebook group so I communicate&nbsp;
with readers there there's all different avenues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:57.360
your goal with Tokyo Black was to make millions&nbsp;
and retire on a beach somewhere I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.880
isn't that what every writer tells himself right&nbsp;
no I maybe I said that in interviews but my goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:10.980
was definitely to finish it finish a book and then&nbsp;
when I published it I just wanted people to enjoy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:16.860
it I really wanted people like I said I wanted&nbsp;
people to feel like they had gone to Japan or feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:21.960
like they'd experienced that and to be excited and&nbsp;
it's an action book so of course I wanted people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:28.560
to get pumped and I remember I remember when the&nbsp;
reviews for Tokyo Black started coming in that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.660
really nerve-wracking actually and this is sort of&nbsp;
part of the process so with with reviews on Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:40.800
you sort of need to kind of jump start it a little&nbsp;
bit because probably only about one percent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.820
people that read your book are going to leave a&nbsp;
review that it's not a high percentage of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:50.400
that go back and do that so what you do is you you&nbsp;
build up a review team of advanced readers where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.680
you're like okay people who have joined your list&nbsp;
like I said you build that mailing list and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:02.100
you say hey my book is going to launch on this&nbsp;
day here's an advanced copy can you please leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:05.640
your honest review whether you like it whether&nbsp;
you don't like it whatever you think just leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:13.140
it on Amazon on this day so you kind of know when&nbsp;
the reviews are going to start to come in because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:18.480
you've told everybody and I remember I was I&nbsp;
hadn't I hadn't gone through that because with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.820
Devil's Due that I there was no review team like&nbsp;
that was the first thing I launched I didn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:28.140
a mailing list or anything I was building it so I&nbsp;
and I hadn't really thought about that then when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:32.100
I launched Tokyo Black and the launch day came&nbsp;
I was like oh my God like I'm gonna get reviews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:37.140
like what if people don't like it and I remember&nbsp;
the reading the reviews that they were coming in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:44.160
